<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/the-moon-and-eclipses">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon is the sky's great teacher — close enough to watch its geometry play out monthly,
    and the only other world humans have walked on. Its <strong>phases</strong> are not Earth's
    shadow (the most common misconception in astronomy) but simple viewing angles of a
    half-lit ball. Its <strong>eclipses</strong>, though, are shadows — spectacular ones,
    possible only because the Sun is about 400× wider than the Moon and also about 400× farther
    away, making the two discs almost exactly the same size in our sky. Between those headlines
    sits a whole course in planetary science: tides, orbital mechanics, and a geological record
    of the solar system's violent youth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four facts that explain the Moon's entire public life" widget="equationStep" config={moonCfg} />

  <h2>The lunar disc: what you actually see</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon is nearly spherical (1,737 km radius), and its face is a two-terrain map. The
    bright, cratered <strong>highlands</strong> are the original crust — anorthosite, rock
    floating to the top of a molten Moon — pocked by 4 billion years of impacts. The dark
    <strong>maria</strong> ("seas", a name from early telescopic observers who mistook them for
    water) are vast basins punched by giant impacts around 4 billion years ago, later flooded
    by basalt lava that welled up and froze smooth. Maria cover about 16% of the surface —
    mostly the near side, which is why the far side, first photographed by the Soviet probe
    Luna 3 in 1959, looks so different: almost all highlands, pocked and pale. Bright rays
    around craters like Tycho are ejecta splashed across the surface, and they fade with age —
    a crude clock read from any back-garden telescope. Between the terrains lies the
    <strong>terminator</strong>, the day–night line, where low sunlight casts long shadows that
    make relief visible: observe there, not at full moon, when overhead light flattens
    everything.
  </p>

  <h2>Synchronous rotation and libration: why "the dark side" doesn't exist</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon's <strong>rotation period equals its orbital period</strong> — 27.3 days — the
    condition called <strong>synchronous rotation</strong> or tidal locking. It is not a
    coincidence but an inevitability: Earth's tides on the early Moon braked its spin until the
    fastest-rotating state that could survive was the one where the same face always points at
    the brake. (Earth is still being braked by the Moon's tides — the day lengthens by about
    2 ms per century — and in tens of billions of years Earth would lock to the Moon too.)
    The far side is therefore the <em>far</em> side, not a dark one: it gets exactly the same
    sunlight, only unseen from Earth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Locking is imperfect in instructive ways. Because the Moon's orbit is elliptical, it moves
    slightly faster at perigee than its rotation carries it, and slower at apogee — letting us
    peek alternately around each eastern and western edge (a longitude libration of about ±8°).
    Its rotation axis is tilted 1.5° to its orbit, tilting the poles in and out of view (±6.7°
    in latitude). And being lifted from horizon to overhead lets observers at different times
    see slightly around the edges. Add them up and <strong>about 59% of the lunar surface is
    visible from Earth over time</strong> — a slowly wobbling face, invisible unless you watch
    for it, predicted by the geometry and confirmed by watching crater positions.
  </p>

  <h2>Sizes and distances: the cosmic coincidence</h2>
  <p>
    Scale the system honestly: the Moon's diameter is 3,474 km — just over a quarter of
    Earth's — and its distance is about 384,400 km, which is 30 Earth diameters away. Most
    diagrams lie about this; the real system is mostly empty space. The Sun, by contrast, is
    1.39 million km across at 149.6 million km. The remarkable accident: Sun diameter ÷ Moon
    diameter ≈ 400, and Sun distance ÷ Moon distance ≈ 390 — so both discs subtend about
    half a degree, and the Moon can cover the Sun <em>exactly</em>. No other planet in the
    solar system enjoys this near-equality — seen from Jupiter, the Sun appears only a fifth
    the width it does from Earth, and Jupiter's big moons would cross it as dots, not discs —
    and the match is temporary: the Moon recedes, so far-future total eclipses will end, and
    eventually only annular ones will remain.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides: the Moon's grip on the ocean</h2>
  <p>
    Gravity weakens with distance, so the Moon pulls the ocean's near side more strongly than
    Earth's centre, and Earth's centre more strongly than the far side. That
    <em>difference</em> — the differential pull — stretches the ocean into two bulges: one
    facing the Moon, one opposite. Earth spins beneath them daily, so most coasts see two high
    tides about 12.4 hours apart. The Sun contributes a tidal pull about 46% as strong; when
    Sun and Moon align (new and full moon) their bulges add — the higher <strong>spring
    tides</strong>; when they pull at right angles (first and last quarter) they partially
    cancel — <strong>neap tides</strong>. The rhythm runs on a 14.8-day beat, and the two
    components' slightly different periods (12.42 vs 12.00 hours) make the tide curve beat in
    and out over the day:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="two tide components beating together — the pattern any coastal tide table repeats" widget="plotter" config={tideCfg} />

  <p>
    Tidal friction does more than make tide tables. It transfers Earth's spin to the Moon's
    orbit: the day lengthens (~2 ms/century), and the Moon recedes <strong>3.8 cm per
    year</strong> — measured by bouncing lasers off the retroreflectors Apollo astronauts left
    on the surface, the most precise Earth–Moon distance measurements anywhere. Rotate the
    clock back: 1.4 billion years ago the day lasted about 18 hours; further back still, the
    Moon hung much closer and the tides were enormous.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Moon's gravity pulls the ocean toward it, so there is
    one bulge." One pull would make one bulge — but tides come from the
    <em>difference</em> in pull across Earth, which necessarily makes two. The far-side bulge
    is the clue students must be able to explain: that water is "left behind" less strongly
    pulled than the Earth beneath it, so it lags outward relative to the planet's centre.</p>

  <h2>Eclipses: geometry at its most dramatic</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>solar eclipse</strong> happens at new moon, when the Moon's shadow touches Earth.
    The dark core of the shadow (the <strong>umbra</strong>) is only a few hundred kilometres
    wide at Earth's surface — total eclipses are local and brief (a maximum of about 7½
    minutes) — while the surrounding <strong>penumbra</strong> gives a partial eclipse across a
    region thousands of kilometres wide. Because the Moon's distance varies from about 356,500
    to 406,700 km while the umbra's tip only just reaches Earth, sometimes the umbral tip falls
    short and the Moon leaves a ring of Sun visible: an <strong>annular</strong> ("ring of
    fire") eclipse. A <strong>lunar eclipse</strong> is the reverse: at full moon the Moon
    passes through Earth's much wider shadow and dims to copper-red — sunlight refracted
    through Earth's atmosphere, literally the light of every sunrise and sunset on Earth at
    once, bent onto the Moon's face. Lunar eclipses last hours and are visible from the whole
    night hemisphere — which is why any given location sees many more lunar than solar ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why not every month? Because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° to the ecliptic. New and
    full moons usually pass above or below the Sun–Earth line. Eclipses require the Moon to be
    at a <strong>node</strong> — where the two orbital planes cross — at the same time it is new
    or full. That triple coincidence recurs in seasons: eclipse seasons twice a year, and the
    whole pattern repeats with the <strong>Saros cycle</strong> of about 18 years 11 days 8
    hours, known to Babylonian astronomers who could — and did — predict eclipses without any
    theory of gravity at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Precession: the wandering pole</h2>
  <p>
    Earth's rotation axis is tilted 23.4°, and the Moon and Sun tug on that equatorial bulge
    like a slow hand on a spinning top: the axis <strong>precesses</strong>, tracing a circle
    of radius 23.4° on the sky once every <strong>25,772 years</strong>. The consequence is a
    drifting pole star: Polaris holds the post today (about 0.7° from the pole), but in about
    12,000 years Vega will be the pole star, and Thuban — the pole star when Egyptians built
    the pyramids — gets another turn. Precession also shifts the position of the equinoxes
    along the zodiac by about 50 arcseconds per year, which is why astrological "star signs"
    no longer match the constellation the Sun actually rises in — and why astronomers must
    quote coordinates for a standard epoch. The same gravitational bookkeeping applies to the
    Moon: its orbit's nodes precess in 18.6 years, the cycle that modulates when eclipses can
    happen and feeds the Saros.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside the Moon, and where it came from</h2>
  <p>
    Seismometers left by Apollo recorded moonquakes for years, giving the Moon an internal
    profile to compare with Earth's: a thin crust (about 34–43 km on average, thicker on the
    far side), a solid mantle, and a small core — roughly 330 km in radius, about 20% of the
    Moon's radius and just 1–2% of its mass, partly molten. Earth's core is over half our
    radius and a third of our mass. That iron-poverty is the single most telling clue to the
    Moon's origin: whatever made the Moon took little of Earth's iron but lots of its rocky
    mantle.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modern answer is the <strong>Giant Impact Hypothesis</strong>: about 4.5 billion years
    ago, a Mars-sized protoplanet (nicknamed Theia) struck the young Earth a glancing blow;
    the debris — mostly mantle rock from both bodies — settled into orbit and accreted into the
    Moon within centuries to millennia. The hypothesis earns its status by passing tests its
    rivals fail. Against <em>co-accretion</em> (Moon forming alongside Earth): the Moon's low
    iron and Earth's iron-rich core don't match two siblings condensing from the same cloud.
    Against <em>capture</em>: no plausible trajectory slows a passing body into orbit without
    stealing extravagant energy. Against <em>fission</em> (Moon spun off fast-spinning Earth):
    the system's angular momentum is far too small. The impact hypothesis explains all three —
    iron sank with Theia's core into Earth, the mantle debris made the Moon, and simulations
    reproduce the angular momentum we observe. One residue keeps research honest: Earth and
    Moon rocks have nearly <em>identical</em> oxygen isotope ratios, harder to arrange if much
    of the Moon came from Theia — current models solve it with a hot, well-mixed post-impact
    Earth or a Theia surprisingly similar to Earth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reaching the Moon is an exercise in energy budgeting. Escaping Earth's gravity well to the
    Moon needs about 3.2 km/s of delta-v beyond low Earth orbit; Apollo missions crossed in
    roughly three days on a <strong>free-return trajectory</strong> — a figure-eight path that
    returns home automatically if the braking burn fails (the design that saved Apollo 13).
    Modern small missions trade time for energy, using <strong>low-energy transfers</strong>
    that ride the Sun–Earth gravitational terrain for weeks or months, arriving with a fraction
    of the fuel. The Moon is close — but "close" in space means three days of coasting at
    39,000 km/h, still the farthest any human has travelled.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon is also a scientific instrument. With no weather and no plate tectonics, its
    surface preserves a 4-billion-year impact record that Earth's geology erases — crater
    counting on lunar terrains calibrates the impact chronology used across the whole inner
    solar system, and the 382 kg of Apollo rocks (1969–72) still anchor the dating of
    planetary surfaces everywhere. The Moon's stabilising role matters to us directly: its
    gravity keeps Earth's axial tilt steady within about a degree over millions of years, and
    without it chaotic tilts would swing climates wildly (Mars, moonless, has wobbled by tens
    of degrees). Meanwhile the far side has become prime real estate for radio astronomy: it
    faces away from Earth's cacophony of radio noise, shielded from human transmitters — China's
    Chang'e 4 landed there in 2019, and low-frequency antennas on the far side are proposed
    windows onto the universe's "dark ages" before the first stars. The nearest world, it
    turns out, is also the quietest radio site in the inner solar system — and a place where
    the next generation of instruments may hear signals older than any star.
  </p>
